bevy-horizon: NAK_DEBUG=xbrk, BRKs that leave scoreboards in flight

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at d4a00ea0ab3f59afb967cc5d779e4263d237bd77, after 0001 to 0006. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies
it; this text before the first "diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

0004's NAK_DEBUG=xbar keeps scoreboards in flight across forward BRA and SYNC, but every BRK
still waits on all of them. Before Turing, NVK turns a texture lookup whose descriptor may differ
across the warp into a loop over the distinct descriptors (nvk_shader.c,
nir_lower_non_uniform_access), and each pass of it ends in a BRK straight after the texture
instruction. So each such lookup waits out its own latency before the next can start.

NAK_DEBUG=xbrk, with xbar, lets a BRK that ends its block and goes only forward keep them in
flight as well, and the wait moves to the first use after the loop. The lanes that break write
only their own lanes, and so do the lanes still looping: the argument xbar already makes for the
two sides of a divergent if. CONT, EXIT and a BRK whose target is laid out before it still wait
on everything, and xbar_verify() checks the result as before.

On Skate 3's world fragment shader (SM53, xbar,sink,remat, SKATE_WORLD_PAGE_ARRAY), the
instruction count (2295) and static cycles (23749) don't change, since NAK's estimate leaves out
scoreboard waits. None of its 12 BRKs waits now, where each waited before, and on the path that
a third of the world takes, a warp on one page has its four lookups in flight at once rather than
one after another. No shader failed to compile or verify on the PC (nakhost, SM53, debug build
with assertions), and calc_instr_deps.rs gains two tests (test_xbar_brk and
test_xbar_brk_backward_waits). Not yet run on hardware; off unless set.

